{"data":{"repository":{"p4539":{"body":"[hermit2, hermit-in-podman, gpt-6.1-sol, host-redacted, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nDocument an offline image recipe and the measured rootless Podman settings for\nrunning Hermit inside a container. The guide explains the inherited-seccomp\nrefusal, host prerequisites and preflight, strict ptrace/KVM namespace setup,\nand the single-process scope observed for DBT and LiteInst. Link it from the\nREADME and include the custom seccomp profile from the refusal matrix.\n\nOwner: coord2-container-docs (codex, local) since 2026-10-20T14:40:24Z\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe guide fixes the epoch and separates non-strict stdout repetition, strict\nruns, and canonical full-observation verification. Measurements use an outer\nnetwork-disabled container. Optional backend results identify the older\nfeature-enabled source and preserve the unsupported combined-workload results.\nThe custom-filter unsafe override is explicit and its example is unmeasured.\nNo runtime policy, assertion, comparator, or test gate changes.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nThis change documents existing syscall, namespace, device, and host policy\nrequirements. It changes no guest syscall result, signal behavior, filesystem\nsemantics, or backend implementation. The writable system mount is explained\nalongside its measured need and reduced outer path protection.\n\n## Validation\n\nSigned head: `e5fb4ecc90e49c1422fd5cb84f53d6fa75690699`.\nBase: `ae5614b0b5886e1f96b7da8b96265f8041b5b577`.\nSignature verified; fingerprint `49F2D1CB86447DFD1F14B27A8B61A0894BDB3A92`.\n\n| Exact-head command | Exit | Time |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `./ci/prepare-rust-scripts.sh` | 0 | 8.571 s |\n| `./scripts/check-portable-paths.sh` | 0 | 4.294 s |\n| `./scripts/check-doc-bare-urls.rs` | 0 | 0.242 s |\n\nPreparation built 37 entrypoints and 26 test harnesses. The bare-URL check\ncovered 342 documented Rust source files. The copied profile parses as JSON and\nmatches the measured profile bytes; the staged diff had no whitespace errors.\nThe committed working tree is clean.\n\nContainer measurements use Hermit 0.5.0 for ptrace/KVM and the documented\nfeature-enabled validate source for optional backends. All seven CLI selectors\nhave permission-matrix results. Strict ptrace and KVM runs completed the\ncombined probe twice with equal raw stdout; DBT and LiteInst completed a\nseparate single-process smoke. The guide records the failing combined guests\nand absent SaBRe/e9patch assets. Docker examples are untested. These\nmeasurements do not establish canonical full-observation parity.\n\nOnly the host field of the identity disclosure is redacted for public output.\n"},"p4677":{"body":"[hermit2, sub-compression-content, gpt-6-astra, role=impl]\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nThe compression integration test now explicitly selects `COMPRESSION_SOURCE_IDENTITY=content` alongside its prebuilt `HERMIT_BIN`. This lets the existing test run from staged sources without Git metadata or Cargo, without adding a generic per-case environment mechanism.\n\nThe fixture's content mode records SHA-256 identities for the actual runner, Hermit binary and compression tools. Schema 1 omits `repository_commit` and records `repository_commit_source=unavailable-staged`. Standalone `run.sh` still defaults to revision mode, preserving Git discovery and explicit `COMPRESSION_SOURCE_REVISION` metadata.\n\nAn inherited `COMPRESSION_SOURCE_REVISION` remains present and conflicts with the caller's explicit content mode, so the runner rejects it. Invalid identity settings, unusable explicit binaries and file-hash failures also remain failures. The hashes identify observed files; they do not archive sources or capture the complete runtime environment.\n\nOwner: sub-compression-content (codex, host withheld) since 2026-10-25T01:49Z\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe strict-launch helper, literal input generator, workload and summary remain byte-identical to the original base. Each attempt still makes 15 strict calls over three repetitions, requires multi-block recovery, compares decoded bytes, compares compressed/recovery hashes across runs, and propagates failures. The Rust caller adds only its explicit identity choice and a comment; input size, retry attempts, budgets, epoch, arguments and result assertions are unchanged.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo syscall, scheduler, backend or guest-visible Linux behavior changes. The changes select fixture provenance and record evidence metadata; no kernel behavior is being reimplemented.\n\n## Validation\n\nHead: `b27adde4bca84c25a190bb312f141b627ca63fdf`.\nBase: `fd04dec46cc92ae22ebcfc6676589ec7ec109742`.\nThe branch contains the reviewed runner commit `7c38b6a4a9e605a22cbf8601c827ce57381dc44d` followed by the separate caller commit. Both signatures verify; the reviewed commit was not rewritten.\n\n- At the new head, focused `rustfmt --check --config skip_children=true hermit-cli/tests/compression.rs`, `git diff --check`, normal commit hooks and signature verification passed. The follow-up changes only that Rust file by two added lines. It was inspected in full.\n- The runner remains byte-identical to the reviewed version, SHA-256 `107a45cebee273ce27e51eca9d0cf918738ca0e206f123eb57ecff4e92927ef0`.\n- The earlier 25 external fixture controls passed at `7c38b6a4a9e605a22cbf8601c827ce57381dc44d` (5.344 seconds summed subprocess wall time). Those controls used a fake Hermit shim and ordinary host compression tools; they qualify the unchanged script's content mode, three runs/15 calls, multi-block recovery, byte/hash comparisons, invalid inputs and failure-status propagation. They were not rerun for this caller change and did not execute the Rust integration test.\n- Independent source/control review approved `7c38b6a4a9e605a22cbf8601c827ce57381dc44d` (report SHA-256 `d3cac04eee43a4949cac61fa29e439d071b805570de3470c415994743315b8f3`). A separate independent review approved the exact caller follow-up `b27adde4bca84c25a190bb312f141b627ca63fdf` with all original assertions preserved (report SHA-256 `d293aab4618ee26e96518315abd1bfb2eda54c9095d9ac00254a4e528c53e0d4`). Both reviews are source/control approvals; neither claims real Hermit execution.\n\nNo Rust caller execution, real Hermit run, full validation receipt, backend qualification, or L0–L4 assurance is claimed. Coordinator publication and exact-head full validation remain pending.\n"},"p4561":{"body":"[hermit2, qemu-rcb, claude-opus-5-5, devbig030, role=impl]\n\nOwner: qemu-rcb (claude, devbig030) since 2026-10-22T02:01Z\n\n**DRAFT for review** (coord2 relaying an owner priority: improvements for pinpointing reproducible-build divergence, item 3).\n\n## Why\n\nA full `--log=info` log of a real build step is large: 817 MB for one 116-package dnf transaction. So nobody keeps it on in every build, and when a build that ran without it does not reproduce, the evidence is gone. That is the TTS CVM INSTALLTIME drift today. Hermit had no bounded in-memory log; CI's `--log-tail-bytes` covers guest logs only.\n\n## What\n\n`hermit --log-ring=SIZE --log-ring-file=FILE run ...` keeps the newest SIZE bytes of hermit's log in FILE, at the `--log` level or INFO if that is quieter.\n\n- FILE is a 64-byte header plus a SIZE-byte circular data area, mapped shared into hermit. Each tracing record is copied in as `[u32 length][bytes]`.\n  - The header's end position moves only after a copy, and the oldest position moves before anything is overwritten.\n  - So the file holds the newest whole records however hermit ends: normal exit, error, panic, the container processes' `_exit`, or SIGKILL. Nothing has to run at exit.\n- Other logging (`--log-file` or stderr) is unchanged; the ring is a second tracing layer with its own filter.\n- Like `--log-file`, FILE is opened on the host before the container exists.\n- `hermit log-diff FILE` decodes a ring file into a log, starting with a DEBUG record naming how many older records were dropped. `hermit log-diff A B` compares ring files with each other or with ordinary logs.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `log_ring::tests::the_file_keeps_the_newest_whole_records_in_order`: 2000 records into a 4 KiB ring; the decoded file is exactly the newest whole records, and the header reports the dropped count.\n- `log_ring::tests::oversized_records_keep_their_tail_and_other_files_are_not_rings`: a record longer than the ring keeps its tail; a non-ring file is passed through; a ring under 4 KiB is refused.\n- `log_ring_keeps_the_end_of_the_info_log_and_log_diff_reads_it` (cli): two runs of `/bin/true` with `--log=info --log-file` and a 16 KiB ring. The ring's records, printed by `log-diff FILE`, are exactly the end of the full log's; the file is 16 KiB + 64 bytes; the two runs' rings compare equal.\n- By hand, through `bin/safehermit`:\n  - With a 64 KiB ring, `ls /etc` kept its last 342 lines, including the run summary.\n  - After SIGKILL of a running hermit (exit 137), a 16 KiB ring held whole records up to syscall #15208.\n- clippy and rustfmt are clean. Full validation has not been run.\n\n## Cost\n\nOne memory copy of each formatted record at the ring's level. The formatting is the cost `--log=info` already has: it is skipped today when nothing logs at INFO, and the ring turns it on. Measuring that cost on a real build is part of the always-on trace design (item 4); this PR does not claim it.\n"},"p4707":{"body":"[hermit2, sub-fifo-diagnostic-local, gpt-6-astra, host-withheld, role=impl]\n\nOwner: sub-fifo-diagnostic-local (codex, host-withheld) since 2026-10-25T20:19Z\n\n## Plain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nFIFO verification test timeouts now include the command's actual status and at most 8192 trailing stderr bytes, or an explicit read error. The existing assertions, 170/180-second deadlines, exit-status checks, guest selection, and verification comparisons are unchanged.\n\n## Determinism\n\nThe diagnostic runs only after an existing deadline assertion fails. It reads captured stderr using file metadata, a seek to `len.saturating_sub(8192)`, and `take(8192)`. It does not change guest execution or comparison semantics.\n\n## Linux Semantics\n\nNo product syscall, mount, FIFO, or scheduling semantics change. This adds evidence to existing test failures.\n\n## Validation\n\nSigned commit `1e806b7cb111e836481719fab71965480a59205a`, based on `c7e0319511a21d76a23ac57afe5fecb6758a1d37`, has source tree `3cbaadb3f17afc8367c451e0fe5a89353822db06`.\n\nOn that exact staged source tree before signing, `cargo check --locked --offline -p hermit-run --test verify_claim_names_its_limit` passed in 51.57 seconds. The pinned-nightly format check and `git diff --check` passed. Independent source review approved the same tree with no findings or goalpost moving. The signed commit's tree, index, and working source match that compiled and reviewed source; compilation was not repeated after signing.\n\nThe first normal commit attempt failed when the pin check's `git grep` reported it could not create a thread under the task limit. That failed attempt remains failed. A separately authorized attempt used command-local `git -c grep.threads=2`; every unchanged normal hook passed, the commit was signed, and signature verification passed. No check was bypassed or edited.\n\nAll ten previously failing FIFO cases remain FAIL/UNKNOWN. No runtime tests were rerun, and no runtime cause or repair is claimed.\n"},"p4668":{"body":"[hermit2, sub-native-pmu, claude-opus-5-5, hostname omitted, role=impl] (manual disclosure; the configured identity resolver reported UNRESOLVED)\n\nOwner: sub-native-pmu (claude, host omitted) since 2026-10-24T18:16:00Z\n\nPlain Language Summary and Project Impact\n\nHermit schedules at retired-conditional-branch (RCB) coordinates. Histories did not identify their counter definition, so replay could consume incompatible coordinates. Reverie's taken-only event on seven Ice Lake-derived models was one mismatch. This PR pins that correction and checks standalone preemption/callback histories' RCB identity before guest launch:\n- New histories are written in an `rcb_history` envelope that names the producer's identity (`rcb:cond-retired:user:v1`, or unqualified with a reason).\n- A run qualifies its clock (backend contract + host PMU profile + installed PMU state, checked against the host's prepared claim), admits every executable input, pins the clock key once, and only then builds the scheduler and writer.\n- A history with no identity, an unqualified identity, or a different metric is refused with a named reason. Analyze, minimize and bisect keep the source identity. The verifier compares inside the envelope.\n- The record epoch, highest-shipped floor, history entry and current-version assertions move together from 0x186 to the reserved 0x187. Old-version refusal fixtures remain intentional.\n- The capability handling adopts Reverie's first typed capability, `rcb_clock_contract`, without loosening any check. The v1 DBT envelope now refuses an omitted key, as its decoder already documented. The wire fingerprint carries the field's width. The golden table names ptrace's contract.\n\nAvailability restriction: history admission is limited to qualified ptrace producer clocks and a replay run with a qualified clock. Missing, unqualified or incompatible clock identities are refused before guest launch. Legacy history files without recorded clock identity are refused; qualified envelopes using the legacy scheduler mode remain admissible. This intentionally reduces the histories and backend/PMU combinations that can be replayed. The coordinator accepted this narrower availability scope; it is not a claim that every backend or PMU profile has been qualified.\n\nCommits\n1. ea55ce1e2fe07fa1806f3856dfd824611641cdcf: Pin the original Reverie PMU chain, with the capability adaptation and counts.\n2. aecb2f07e243c815776a200ec71749dee438aa60: Refuse RCB histories this run cannot execute (guard, epoch 0x187, fixture and verifier repairs, counts).\n\n3. b9e51c0b149c0787843b3fc76226e607524c4c52: Decode all seven emitted-history integration readers through the production Recording API.\n4. c53927b70a4404ebcb593b4f7eb0d31e996635b3: Pin the exact catalog-corrected Reverie revision everywhere.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (not merge-ready until both land):\n- Reverie 52640bca..d9487fbc (four commits on Reverie main 8b7ab5f3, author and committer hermit-bot3), https://github.com/rrnewton/reverie/pull/1124, open. That PR is separate. On landing, the pin moves to the landed SHA.\n- Land https://github.com/rrnewton/hermit/pull/4657 first, then remove its duplicate ec42ae2e carry when rebasing this PR. ec42ae2e carries c36b658e, including its post-974243e3 corrections, and matches the published priority head 946ab50d's non-count delta. Preserve newer priority-successor repairs when reconciling. After that, coordinate the Reverie dependency and this complete Hermit consumer, with no independent Hermit pin advance into the corrected event table between them. Never land the pin-only ea55ce1e separately.\n\nAt landing, re-read main's highest-shipped record version. If another landing has consumed 0x187, this combined change takes HIGHEST_SHIPPED+1, updating the version, floor, history and all current-version assertions together and repeating the old-literal test audit.\n\nHuman Review Required: proposed trigger 3 (a new determinization strategy: refusing histories by RCB clock identity before replay). For the coordinator to confirm or reject.\n\nDeterminism\n\nQualification and admission read only the history file, the backend's stated contract, CPUID and the process PMU state, and publish nothing. The one mutation, recording the clock key with `commit_pin`, happens once before this run's scheduler, writer and guest are created. A refusal happens before guest launch. The guard checks compatibility before scheduling; the pinned Reverie change also corrects the underlying counter event and timer initialization, so this combined PR can change execution on affected hosts.\n\nLinux Semantics\n\nThe new history refusals are Hermit policy applied before launch. The pinned counter fix remains `perf_event_open` PERF_TYPE_RAW, user mode only. This handoff does not claim that affected runs have unchanged timing or schedules.\n\n\nReview correction\n\nSeven integration read sites now decode the format-2 recording through Recording::read/decode and into_legacy before inspecting its inner record. All original epoch, priority, preemption-count, latency and nonleader-exec predicates remain. Inspection JSON is never written as an executable history; bounded nonleader reads and validation remain. No test selector, ignore, fixture, timeout or tolerance changes.\n\nValidation at signed c53927b70a4404ebcb593b4f7eb0d31e996635b3, pin d9487fbcab15d758c4e02be4f479b5f1a0f7d420\n\n- Independent source reviews approve the seven reader repairs and paired pin update. Final-head fmt --all --check and package/test Clippy with -D warnings have rc0.\n- Full pinned test.cli: 611/611 pass at actual committed width 16, zero retries, 65.872s test / 75.126s node time, unchanged 6 GiB/900s caps; 130 ordinary selection exclusions. The earlier width-1 diagnostic also passed 611/611 in 811.847s, with one recorded skid-overshoot retry.\n- Maintained pinned regular/integration nodes: 2549/2549 pass at width 8, including all 17 identities that failed in the hosted environment. Shell replay retains the same 22s CPU bound. All four requested unignored clock_nanosleep/nanosleep chaos races, all 8 clock_determinism and 14 chaos_sched_yield_progress cases pass. All seven repaired reader sites are exercised across these suites and the PMU selection.\n- The 14-node PMU selection passes, including all 16 PMU CLI cases without retries. Hardware evidence is AMD family 0x1a/model 0x11 only; it does not qualify corrected Intel profiles.\n- Earlier hosted suites passed 2518/2535: 13 fixture/dependency failures, one failed chroot prerequisite, three unresolved execution/replay failures. Two hosted CLI runs OOM-aborted at their unchanged 6 GiB cap, with only 64/63 unique completed passes. These failed observations remain. The pinned passes establish neither their causes nor unchanged-base failure; the hosted OOM remains unexplained.\n\nThese are scoped, off-the-record branch results, not a canonical full-validation receipt. The full Reverie ancestry gate remains unpassed while the paired pin is unlanded. Earlier failed preparation attempts remain recorded. Both main landings stay held pending dependency reconciliation and review.\n\nHistorical aecb build/unit/discovery, selftest and offline-pin receipts remain earlier-head evidence. A separate native full sweep remains failed: service 3917 passed versus author 3911, 3 failed/1 fatal/2 skipped, built tree unbound; its real_random readv deadline failure is unexplained. Later focused passes do not replace it.\n\nFollow-up scope\n\nThe shared cross-family metric still needs a measured equivalence witness; Alder Lake 0x9A and model0x86 remain unqualified. Full hermit record/replay metadata does not yet carry this identity. The e9patch preprocessing-under-ptrace path needs its capability declarations reconciled. These remain documented limitations, not claims of qualification; issue URLs will be added from the four queued actions.\n"}}}}herdr-run: retired idle tab wF:t1M1 (pane wF:p1M1, agent musetrain, last run 2026-11-02T01:22:50Z) idle past the 300s TTL
